WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.430
Intelligent local model routing
is coming to GitHub Copilot.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.090
A few weeks ago,
we announced Project HydraFusion,

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.770
an intelligent model orchestration
that plans, implements,

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.650
and executes across a wide
range of cloud models,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.730
giving you frontier-level performance

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:20.810
while significantly
reducing cost, up to 67%.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:26.210
But our vision goes beyond just the models
that are available in the cloud,

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.070
because we think that unlimited

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.510
and unmetered intelligence
belongs on every desktop.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.350
This is why we're pleased
to announce that soon,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:42.150
Copilot will intelligently route to and
leverage local models on capable devices.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.430
First, you'll no longer have to configure
local models in GitHub Copilot itself.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.810
Any models that you have installed
with supported providers,

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.750
such as Microsoft Foundry Local or Ollama,

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.890
will just automatically be discovered
and will appear in the model picker.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.090
Capable devices will also be able
to install MAI Code 1.1 Flash,

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.250
a local model available for install
right from GitHub Copilot.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.150
Copilot will then automatically use
these models when set to auto-routing.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.010
There are a myriad of scenarios
where local models are perfect,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.450
and the inference
is completely free for you.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.390
For instance, when set to auto-routing,

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.590
Copilot can automatically offload
to a local model for simple tasks,

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.050
such as explaining how a codebase
works or how to run a project.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.110
You can also plan with large
frontier models, such as Astra,

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.970
and then hand off to a local model
to implement while you sleep.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.210
And users love automations
in GitHub Copilot.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.450
These are tasks that run
on a schedule in the background.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.790
Local models work incredibly here,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.090
able to work on very long-running tasks

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:54.430
and costing you exactly
zero AI credits to use.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.410
You can also go completely offline and
still have access to your local models,

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.490
which is perfect for working disconnected

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.630
or when you just don't have
an internet connection.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.110
Project HydraFusion is available
today in GitHub Copilot.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.550
You can look for it under
experimental settings.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.690
And capable devices will be able

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.510
to use intelligent local
model routing very soon,

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.690
including MAI Code 1.1 Flash.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:24.870
With GitHub Copilot, you get
intelligent multi-model routing,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.230
both in the cloud and very soon,

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.730
with local models that run on your machine

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.730
and cost you exactly
zero dollars and zero cents.

